  ;;
  ;; All SEVEN heads of the ADR's set are here as of 2026-09-06. The last
  ;; three were held back by three claims that turned out, on measurement, to
  ;; be about the claims rather than about the language:
  ;;
  ;;   handle  was "a guest could intercept its own capability calls". It
  ;;           cannot: a cap-call contributes no :abort to the effect row, so
  ;;           `(try (cap-call ...) (catch ...))` is refused `try body cannot
  ;;           abort; there is nothing to catch`. The protection is enforced by
  ;;           the row, not by withholding the head.
  ;;   rel     was "no relational value model exists". kgraph-assert! is the
  ;;           language's EAVT store (ADR-2607198300) and is already admitted
  ;;           under :pure-product, so the head adds no authority.
  ;;   query   was the same claim. kgraph-get is a POINT read, which is what
  ;;           `query` is here -- not a pattern query, and the head's own
  ;;           refusal message says so at the wrong arity.
  ;;
  ;; `:backends` is recorded PER HEAD below, because they differ: rel and
  ;; query are KIR-only. That is inherited, not introduced -- `(kgraph-get 1 2)`
  ;; alone fails `:wasm-local-encoding` on wasm32 while a plain program
  ;; compiles (measured 2026-09-06 against amu 9bb5ea68).
  :pure-s-expression-core
  {:desugars-to "each head onto the existing primitive it spells: lam -> the fn lambda lift, app -> ordinary prefix application, ref -> the named symbol, perform -> cap-call"
   :forms [lam app ref perform rel query handle]
   ;; :compiler ONLY, and that was measured rather than assumed the second
   ;; time. The first version of this entry claimed :kotoba-wasm as well.
   ;; Measured 2026-09-06 against kotoba 959aecce: the legacy emitter
   ;; (kotoba.runtime/wasm-binary, which IS what the :kotoba-wasm token
   ;; names) rejects all four heads :unsupported-op -- "app", "lam" and
   ;; "perform" by name. The discriminating control is that four heads which
   ;; DO claim :kotoba-wasm (cond, when, doseq, assert) compile there, and
   ;; :atom-local, which claims :compiler alone, is rejected there. So the
   ;; token means the legacy emitter, and this entry does not have it.
   ;;
   ;; Nothing measured that claim when it landed: `sugar-portability` only
   ;; flags a FULL three-backend claim and `portable-without-evidence` needs
   ;; a :conformance key first, so a two-backend claim with neither is
   ;; checked by nothing. kotoba's `the-legacy-emitter-has-not-widened-to-the-
   ;; pure-heads` now measures this one in both directions.
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler}
   :adr "docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-pure-s-expression-core-and-cljk-surface.md"
   :per-form
   {lam "(lam [params] body) -> (fn [params] body). One clause; the fn ABI's arity bound applies unchanged."
    app "(app f a ...) -> (f a ...). The operator may be a symbol or a computed closure expression; a computed one is let-bound and called by name, which is the shape a lexically bound closure already had."
    ref "(ref name) -> name, and ONLY for a name the module's own top level defines. `ref` is also Clojure's STM constructor, which lang/surface-status.edn :no-ambient-mutation still names in its :surface -- so this is the one head here whose OTHER reading is forbidden. It is not in :forbidden-heads (a head cannot be in the admitted set and that one at once) and is excused instead through :no-ambient-mutation :admitted-via-pure-core-elaboration; the STM refusal lives in the rewrite, which refuses every other shape of (ref ...) with :kotoba.error/ambient-forbidden. Measured 2026-09-06 with `ref` already out of :forbidden-heads: all seven STM spellings are still refused. :spelling-collision carries that evidence."
    perform "(perform :kind/op v ...) -> (cap-call :kind/op v ...). Carries exactly cap-call's authority: same registry resolution, same effect row, same :named-operations, same declare-then-check against the namespace's :capabilities. The numeric wire-id form is NOT admitted -- wire ids are ABI, not source vocabulary. Measured 2026-09-06: byte-identical wasm32 to the cap-call spelling."
    rel "(rel e a v) -> (kgraph-assert! e a v). Exactly 3 arguments. Adds no authority: the primitive is already admitted under :pure-product."
    query "(query e a) -> (kgraph-get e a). Exactly 2 arguments. A POINT read, not a pattern query -- there is no relational pattern form in this language, and the arity refusal says so. It inherits the primitive's sentinel (i64 MIN for an absent entry-attribute) and is deliberately NOT wrapped in an [:option T]: a datom whose value IS i64 MIN is indistinguishable from an absent one, so an option would promise a totality the store does not have."
    handle "(handle body (catch ...)) -> (try body (catch ...)). The elimination form for :abort (lang/abort-ability.edn), which is the one ability a guest can handle -- capability effects are answered by the host and a `handle` CANNOT reach them, because a cap-call contributes no :abort to the row and `try` refuses a body that cannot abort. Measured 2026-09-06: identical :kir-sha256 and identical wasm32 bytes to the `try` spelling. NOTE the asymmetry: `perform` and `handle` are not a matched pair here -- `perform` introduces a capability effect only the host answers, `handle` eliminates the abort ability."}
   ;; `:compiler-kir-only` on rel/query was falsified 2026-09-08: both LOWER,
   ;; to aarch64-macos machine code, and the artifact runs. The wasm32 gap is
   ;; real and stays -- it was the ceiling that got read as a KIR ceiling.
   :backends-per-head
   {lam #{:compiler} app #{:compiler} ref #{:compiler} perform #{:compiler}
    handle #{:compiler} rel #{:compiler} query #{:compiler}}

   ;; The three tokens in `:backends` above name BACKENDS, not amu TARGETS:
   ;; `:compiler` is amu as a whole (its own note says multi-target KIR), and
   ;; `:kotoba-wasm` names the legacy kotoba.runtime/wasm-binary emitter, which
   ;; rejects these heads (:unsupported-op, recorded 2026-09-06 in
   ;; lang/surface-status.edn). Per-target reach therefore CANNOT be said in
   ;; that vocabulary, and saying it there would claim the legacy emitter
   ;; accepts them. It is recorded here instead.
   ;;
   ;; :compiles and :executes are deliberately different values. Building an
   ;; artifact is not running one; a head reaches :executes only after the
   ;; native artifact was run through tools/kexe_loader.c and answered with
   ;; the expected value.
   ;;
   ;; Measured 2026-09-08, amu origin/main a169d7bf, every command --jvm-free.
   :amu-targets-per-head
   {lam     {:wasm32 :compiles :aarch64-macos :executes}
    app     {:wasm32 :compiles :aarch64-macos :executes}
    ref     {:wasm32 :compiles :aarch64-macos :executes}
    ;; native COMPILES but the bare loader traps (SIGTRAP): there is no native
    ;; capability provider. Compiling to a target and running on that host
    ;; face are two claims and only the first holds.
    perform {:wasm32 :compiles :aarch64-macos :compiles-only}
    ;; :verify -- native artifact contains an unsupported effect. INHERITED:
    ;; the same program with plain (try .. (catch ..)) and no `handle` is
    ;; refused with the identical message, so the missing native lowering is
    ;; the :abort ability, not this head.
    handle  {:wasm32 :compiles :aarch64-macos :refused}
    ;; :wasm-local-encoding on wasm32, inherited from the kgraph primitives
    ;; (measured 2026-09-06, amu 9bb5ea68). Native executes: (query e a)
    ;; answered 7 storing 7 and 41 storing 41, so it reads the store.
    rel     {:wasm32 :refused  :aarch64-macos :executes}
    query   {:wasm32 :refused  :aarch64-macos :executes}}
   :note "the source spelling only; no new runtime representation, no new capability, no new head at any backend"}
  :invoke {:desugars-to "arity-specific static closure dispatcher"
           :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
           :note "explicit for computed/non-symbol closure expressions and result-family selection; lexical symbol heads use ordinary application; no host computed call/reflection"}
  :fn-ref {:desugars-to "deterministic closure wrappers for known top-level arities"
           :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
           :note "explicit to avoid lexical symbol-shadowing ambiguity; arities 0..4"}
  :apply {:desugars-to "bounded runtime argument-chain scan plus arity-specific static closure dispatcher"
          :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
          :note "zero to four total arguments; fixed prefixes supported; oversized runtime tails return 0"}
  :eval {:desugars-to "typed-cap-call :code/eval over a bounded document request naming a checked DefCID"
         :level "L4" :backends #{:compiler}
         :request-type :document
         :result-type :infer-from-context
         :authority :host-bound
         :never #{:source-text :reader-eval :ambient-namespace :authority-amplification}}
  :lazy-cons {:desugars-to "resolver closure yielding a pair of zero-arity head and tail closure thunks"
              :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
              :note "head and tail force independently; call-by-name, not memoized; inferred effects must be empty"}
  :lazy-first {:desugars-to "invoke the head thunk" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :lazy-rest {:desugars-to "invoke the tail thunk" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :lazy-empty? {:desugars-to "compare resolved lazy cell with zero" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :lazy-map {:desugars-to "recursive lazy cells with closure-normalized matching-arity callback"
             :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
             :note "one to four sources; named, inline, or stored matching-arity closure callback; shortest termination"}
  :lazy-filter {:desugars-to "resolver-delayed fuel-bounded predicate search"
                :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
                :note "all-rejected finite sources resolve to empty; named, inline, or stored closure predicates"}
  :take {:desugars-to "fuel-bounded lazy forcing into eager pair-chain" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :drop {:desugars-to "fuel-bounded tail forcing" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :count {:desugars-to "bounded pair-chain walk" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :nth {:desugars-to "bounded indexed pair-chain walk" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
        :note "supports optional default"}
  :peek {:desugars-to "empty-safe pair-first" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :pop {:desugars-to "empty-safe pair-second" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :keys {:desugars-to "persistent map-key projection" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :vals {:desugars-to "persistent map-value projection" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  ;; NOT a map filter. Same measurement as :contains? above: `dissoc` had no
  ;; desugar arm either, and was refused `operation has no admitted lowering` on
  ;; every receiver until 2026-09-03.
  :dissoc
  {:desugars-to "typed-map removal, folded left over the keys: (typed-map-dissoc [:map K V] receiver key)"
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
   :admits "a canonical [:map K V] receiver and one or more keys, on every key type the typed map admits"
   :arity "2 or more"
   :refuses
   {:receiver "anything that is not a canonical [:map K V]: a typed set, and the legacy keyword-keyed bounded map"
    :message "dissoc requires a canonical typed map [:map key-type value-type]; got <type>. A typed set answers to typed-set-disj, and the bounded keyword map has no removal primitive at all"
    :code :kotoba.error/map-dissoc-receiver
    :arity "dissoc requires a map and at least one key"}
   :note "reserved as well as implemented: (defn dissoc ...) is refused `reserved function name`"}
  :record-constructor
  {:desugars-to "nominal record constructors ->Type and map->Type"
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
   :note "semantic identity is nominal; the compiler lowers to nominal record descriptors while legacy primary emitters use :kotoba.record/type tagged maps"}
  :protocol-dispatch
  {:desugars-to "closed-world static dispatch plus record-specific functions"
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
   :note "closed semantics fail closed with no reflection: the compiler rejects an unknown or unimplemented receiver statically; primary emitters generate only sealed record cases and trap an unknown tag; neither admits a runtime fallback"}
  :interface-contract
  {:desugars-to "the same safe static method contract as defprotocol"
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
   :note "language interface only; never ambient JVM/JS interop"}
  :protocol-extension
  {:desugars-to "static additions to the closed protocol table"
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs} :forms [extend-type extend-protocol]
   :note "each section implements every method exactly once; canonical extend-protocol/default statically specializes otherwise-unimplemented records in the sealed module and never becomes a dynamic fallback"}
  :keyword-literal {:desugars-to "deterministic FNV-1a integer identity" :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}}
  :portable-string-symbol-values
  {:desugars-to "deterministic tagged integer identity with module collision rejection"
   :level "L2" :backends #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}
   :note "strings are immutable and limited to 127 UTF-8 bytes; literal IDs remain deterministic; primary Wasm dynamic results use bounded heap descriptors; substring indexes are checked UTF-8 byte boundaries"}
  :string-host-arg {:desugars-to "(str-ptr s) (str-len s)" :level "L2" :backends #{:kotoba-wasm}
                    :note "bare string literal in first position of string-head host ops"}
  :data-host-arg {:desugars-to "(bytes-ptr V) (bytes-len V) over canonical kotoba.value.v1 bytes"
                  :level "L2" :backends #{:kotoba-wasm}
                  :adr "ADR-kotoba-canonical-value-codec (VC5)"
                  :note "constant collection literal in first position of data-head host ops; constants only, no runtime construction; leaves the 127-UTF-8-byte portable-string bound and survives into typed KIR as structured data"}}

 ;; Never admit in guest source (subset gate).
 ;; `reset!` and `swap!` joined this set on 2026-08-12. They were already named
 ;; by `:no-ambient-mutation` in surface-status, alongside `atom` and `set!`,
 ;; but only the latter two were listed here -- so the compiler refused
 ;; `(atom x)` with "dynamic loading, interop, mutation, and metaprogramming
 ;; are forbidden" and refused `(reset! x 1)` with "operation has no admitted
 ;; lowering". Same named invariant, two mechanisms, and the second one holds
 ;; only for as long as nothing gives those names a lowering.
 ;; `grammar-authority`'s :forbidden/security-surface-not-forbidden now fails
 ;; closed on that shape.
 ;;
 ;; WHY each head is forbidden is classified in surface-status.edn :invariants
 ;; (:shielding-axis -- :code-identity / :dispatch-bypass / :authority /
 ;; :control-effect-tracking / :resource-bounds -- plus :widening-path where
 ;; the harm is fully covered by grant-intersection dispatch). Authority-axis
 ;; heads relax only when their widening preconditions land (superproject
 ;; adr-2608301500-guest-syntax-restrictions-classified-by-shielding-mechanism).
 ;; `throw try catch` left this set on 2026-09-02 (lang/abort-ability.edn,
 ;; slice 1). They are not ambient any more: the compiler admits them only
 ;; through the `:throw` / `:try` sugar entries above, which lower them to
 ;; `[:result T E]` values and put `:abort` on the inferred effect row. The
 ;; invariant `:explicit-errors` names -- no UNTRACKED control effect -- is
 ;; what that elaboration enforces; the heads are not what it forbids.
 ;; `atom` / `reset!` / `swap!` left this set on 2026-09-02: local-state slice 1
 ;; (lang/local-state.edn) admits a NON-ESCAPING, function-local atom by
 ;; elaboration -- the cell becomes ordinary `let` rebindings, so it does not
 ;; exist at runtime, needs no grant and adds nothing to the effect row. See
 ;; :sugar :atom-local below, and :no-ambient-mutation in surface-status.edn,
 ;; whose invariant is that state must not be AMBIENT rather than that mutation
 ;; is forbidden. `ref` / `dosync` / `volatile!` / `set!` / `binding` / `var` /
 ;; `alter-var-root` stay here: they have no ability model decided.
 :forbidden-heads
 #{load load-file load-string read-string compile require use import ns-resolve resolve alter-var-root
   future pmap agent send send-off new . .. set! defmacro
   locking dosync volatile! binding var}
 ;; `ref` LEFT this set on 2026-09-06, the same way `atom` / `swap!` /
 ;; `reset!` / `deref` left it on 2026-09-02: it is admitted via elaboration,
 ;; in exactly one shape, and a head in this set cannot also be in the
 ;; admitted head set without the document contradicting itself -- which is
 ;; what `kotoba-lang/grammar`'s `the-admission-set-holds-real-heads-and-not-
 ;; feature-names` refused, correctly.
 ;;
 ;; The STM reading is NOT admitted and the refusal did not weaken. It moved
 ;; from this catalog into the elaboration, where the shape can be seen:
 ;; `(ref name)` for a name the module's own top level defines lowers to that
 ;; symbol, and EVERY other shape is refused `:kotoba.error/ambient-forbidden`
 ;; by the rewrite itself. Measured 2026-09-06 against kotoba-sema 04e0c79c
 ;; with `ref` already removed from this set: all seven STM spellings
 ;; (`(ref 0)`, `(ref local)`, deref, dosync, alter, commute, ref-set) are
 ;; still refused with that code. `lang/surface-status.edn`
 ;; `:no-ambient-mutation` keeps `ref` in `:surface`, adds it to
 ;; `:admitted-via-elaboration`, and carries the measurement under
 ;; `:spelling-collision`.

 ;; Arithmetic / comparison / predicates admitted after validation.
 ;; These are the INTEGER heads. Floating point is not spelled `+` and is not
 ;; here; see :floating-point below for why the two sets are separate and what
 ;; the float heads actually are.
 :arithmetic #{+ - * quot bit-xor bit-and bit-or}
 :comparisons #{= < > <= >=}
 :predicates #{not zero? pos? neg? string? symbol? keyword? string-length string=
               string-concat string-substring symbol
               ;; String search surface (kbb scripts-port wave 2): bounded UTF-8
               ;; byte-index operations. string-contains? and string-split-count
               ;; were already typed and evaluated by the compiler and KIR; the
               ;; grammar authority simply did not say so, so every kbb guest
               ;; that needed a substring search hand-rolled a char-at
               ;; recursion (src/edn_validate.kotoba skip-string). All three
               ;; take UTF-8 byte semantics like string-substring; both string
               ;; arguments obey the 127-byte literal bound.
               ;;   (string-index-of haystack needle) -> i64, first UTF-8 byte
               ;;     offset of needle in haystack, -1 when absent (Clojure's
               ;;     .indexOf contract, byte-unit). Empty needle is refused.
               ;;   (string-contains? haystack needle) -> bool.
               ;;   (string-split-count haystack sep) -> i64, non-empty sep,
               ;;     segment count.
               string-index-of string-contains? string-split-count}

 ;; Host-import families that take UTF-8 (ptr,len) as first two wasm params.
 ;; Host-import families whose first argument is STRUCTURED data rather than
 ;; text. A constant collection literal in that position lowers to
 ;; (bytes-ptr V) (bytes-len V) over the canonical kotoba.value.v1 bytes of the
 ;; value -- the same (ptr,len) ABI the string form uses, carrying typed data
 ;; instead of UTF-8 text (ADR-kotoba-canonical-value-codec, VC5).
 ;;
 ;; This admits no new call head: it is the same host op with a
 ;; differently-shaped first argument, and in host-argument position a
 ;; collection literal cannot be anything but data. Only compile-time constants
 ;; are admissible -- quote is not a core special form and keyword literals
 ;; lower to an FNV-1a integer identity, so a guest cannot build an EDN value
 ;; at run time. Capability rules are unchanged: kgraph-query stays denied
 ;; under any scoped grant because a query's identity does not bound which
 ;; entities its join returns.
 :data-head-host-ops
 #{kgraph-assert! kgraph-retract! kgraph-get-objects kgraph-query}

 ;; Bare string literals in source lower to (str-ptr s) (str-len s).
 :string-head-host-ops
 #{kami-nearest-tagged kami-spawn kami-count-tagged kami-axis
   kami-move-tagged-toward! kami-despawn-within!
   audio-play
   nearest-tagged spawn-entity count-tagged axis play-sound
   clipboard-write-str
   http-fetch http-post transport-connect tls-open tls-server-end-point
   keychain-read keychain-write
   fs-read fs-write fs-write-atomic
   sha256-hex log-write log-read
   llm-infer
   ;; kbb ops-script surface (ADR-2607181900 readiness gate): env-read takes
   ;; (name-ptr name-len) — a bare string literal lowers exactly like the
   ;; fs-read path argument above.
   env-read
   ;; fs-browse takes (dir-ptr dir-len) — same bare-string lowering
   ;; (kbb slice 2).
   fs-browse
   ;; fs-browse-dir takes (dir-ptr dir-len) — same bare-string lowering
   ;; (kotoba-lang/find recursive tree-walk, capability id 261).
   fs-browse-dir
   ;; web-wide crawl (com-junkawasaki/root ADR-2607252400)。string-head:
   ;; 先頭2引数が UTF-8 (ptr,len) に lower される。WARC 本文(250KB級)は
   ;; guest に渡さず、cc-warc-extract は CID + 有界サマリだけを返す。
   cc-cdx-query cc-warc-extract corpus-append corpus-publish}

 :admission-limits
 {:max-functions 1024
  :max-parameters 5
  :max-bindings 4096
  :max-symbol-chars 128
  :max-expression-nodes 50000
  :max-list-items 128
  :max-set-items 16
  :max-source-bytes 1048576
  :max-reader-depth 512
  ;; 2026-09-11: the bound on `(:params [...])` in a template module. Eight is
  ;; a bound, not a measurement -- kotoba.set's twelve templates each need one.
  :max-template-parameters 8}

 ;; Author-facing hints when a form is rejected (diagnostics, L2).
 :diagnostic-hints
 {"eval" "typed eval only: request must name checked KIR by DefCID and requires :code/eval authority"
  "require" "forbidden: guests cannot require; use host imports + package-lock (CID packages on L3)"
  "use" "forbidden: same as require — no ambient namespaces in guests"
  "import" "forbidden: no Java/JS interop in guests"
  "set!" "forbidden: no ambient mutation; host owns state"
  "atom" "forbidden: guest is pure; put mutable state on the host"
  "swap!" "forbidden: guest is pure; host owns atoms"
  "reset!" "forbidden: guest is pure; host owns atoms"
  "defmacro" "forbidden: no macros in safe guest surface"
  "try" "admitted (abort slice 1): one body, one (catch [error-type] binder handler) clause; lowers to result-match-of over [:result T E]"
  "catch" "admitted only as the clause of a try"
  "throw" "admitted (abort slice 1): one error value, tail position or let binding value; the function acquires :abort and returns [:result T E]"
  "future" "forbidden: no threads in WASM guest"
  "agent" "forbidden: no agents in WASM guest"
  "new" "forbidden: no interop constructors"
  "." "forbidden: no interop method calls"
  ".." "forbidden: no interop threading"
  "binding" "forbidden: no dynamic var mutation"
  "var" "forbidden: no var objects in guest"
  "loop" "use recursion or compiler loop sugar with fuel; unbounded loops are fuel-trapped"
  "recur" "pair with loop/recursion only inside admitted control forms"
  "map" "portable eager one-to-five-source map and lazy one-to-four-source map; named, inline, and stored closure callbacks"
  "filter" "portable eager and lazy filter with named, inline, or stored closure predicates"
  "reduce" "compiler supports with-init reduce and Clojure-style no-init reduce via inline fn with []/[acc value] arities"
  "set" "the set constructor fn is not portable; use a bounded set literal #{...} on the compiler backend"
  "count" "compiler supports fuel-bounded pair-chain count"
  "nth" "compiler supports pair-chain nth with optional default"
  "keys" "compiler returns an eager persistent pair-chain of map keys"
  "vals" "compiler returns an eager persistent pair-chain of map values"
  "for" "forbidden-style sequence comp; use recursion / host"
  "doseq" "use doseq-entities host bulk op or recursion"
  "str" "for host string args prefer bare string literals (auto str-ptr/str-len) or (str-ptr s)/(str-len s)"
  "println" "no stdout; use log-write host import under policy"
  "print" "no stdout; use log-write host import under policy"}

 :backends
 {:kotoba-wasm
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/kotoba"
   :entry "kotoba.runtime/wasm-binary"
   :notes "primary product emit path for host-import rich guests"}
  :compiler
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/amu"
   :entry "kotoba.compiler.frontend/analyze"
   :notes "deny-by-default multi-target KIR; sugar desugars to pair/if/let/cap-call"}
  :kototama
  {:repo "kotoba-lang/kototama"
   :entry "run already-emitted .wasm"
   :notes "runtime only — does not own grammar"}}

 ;; Builtins admitted without host-import registration (memory/string ABI helpers).
 ;; Use strings for ops whose names are not valid bare EDN symbols (e.g. "/").
 :admitted-builtins
 #{"alloc" "alloc-checked" "str-ptr" "str-len" "bytes-ptr" "bytes-len" "byte-at" "mem-byte-at" "mem-i32-at"
   "byte-store!" "i32-store!" "memory-pages" "memory-grow"
   ;; The "i64*" and "f32*" families below are the LEGACY WASM EMITTER's builtin
   ;; spelling, not the canonical operation vocabulary. The canonical spelling of
   ;; integer addition in this same file is plain `+`, in :arithmetic -- not
   ;; "i64+" -- and the canonical spelling of binary32 addition is `f32-add`,
   ;; which is what `kotoba.compiler.frontend`'s `f32-operations` accepts and
   ;; what `kotoba.kir` evaluates. See :floating-point below and
   ;; docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-floating-point-on-native.md. Neither set is removed:
   ;; removing these would break the legacy emitter, and promoting them would
   ;; give one operation two names.
   "i64" "i64+" "i64-" "i64*" "i64and" "i64or" "i64xor" "i64shl" "i64shr" "i64ushr"
   "f32" "f32+" "f32-" "f32*" "f32/" "f32div" "f32sqrt" "f32neg"
   "f32=" "f32<" "f32>" "f32<=" "f32>="
   "has-capability?" "cap-acquire"
   "result-ok?" "result-err?" "result-write!" "result-status" "result-value"
   "call-indirect" "mod" "rem" "inc" "dec" "min" "max" "/" "bit-or"
   "bit-shift-left" "bit-shift-right" "unsigned-bit-shift-right" "host-i64-roundtrip"
   "pair" "pair-first" "pair-second" "list" "cons" "first" "second" "rest" "empty?"
   "quot" "bit-and" "bit-xor"

   ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
   ;; The kernel families. Admitted as builtins -- no host import, no
   ;; capability grant, no registration -- by `kotoba.compiler.frontend`
   ;; (kotoba-sema), which is the only thing that decides what the COMPILER
   ;; admits.
   ;;
   ;; This set is read in exactly one place: `kotoba.grammar/admitted-heads`
   ;; in kotoba-lang/kotoba's vendored grammar loader, where it feeds
   ;; `strict-problems`' known-head set. So a head missing from here is
   ;; reported by that repository as `:unknown-form` even though the compiler
   ;; admits it, and a head added here stops being reported there -- the
   ;; compiler still decides whether it has a lowering. Nothing in
   ;; kotoba-lang, kotoba-sema or amu reads it at all.
   ;;
   ;; (Measured 2026-09-03. The first draft of this comment said "this set
   ;; decides nothing", from a grep that covered `src test scripts` in each
   ;; repository and not `vendor/` -- which is where the one reader lives.
   ;; Corrected before landing.)
   ;;
   ;; Measured 2026-09-03 against kotoba-sema `1afff23`:
   ;;
   ;;   kernel-memory-operations       53
   ;;   slice-value-operations          8
   ;;   kernel-privileged-operations   53
   ;;                                 ---
   ;;                                 114
   ;;
   ;; and this set named THREE of them -- `kernel-load-u8`, `kernel-store-u8`,
   ;; `kernel-boot-info`. The understatement predates the memwidth wave by
   ;; seven operations: the 4 KiB and 16 KiB tiers, the u32 pair,
   ;; `kernel-subregion` and the lock pair were all shipped on both native
   ;; ISAs without ever reaching this list. An authority that lags its
   ;; frontend is the same defect as one that runs ahead of it, in the other
   ;; direction -- and a reader asking "can Kotoba address a NIC ring?" got
   ;; "no" from the only file that claims to answer.
   ;;
   ;; What each family MEANS is in lang/surface-status.edn `:checked-memory`.
   ;; The privileged family has no surface-status entry yet; that gap is
   ;; recorded there by name rather than left to `:default-for-missing`.

   ;; Checked kernel memory, byte-indexed. Four transfer widths by four window
   ;; tiers. `base length index [value]`; `length` is capped at the tier
   ;; (512 / 4096 / 16384 / 65536) and every access is bounds-checked.
   "kernel-load-u8"       "kernel-store-u8"
   "kernel-load-u8-4k"    "kernel-store-u8-4k"
   "kernel-load-u8-16k"   "kernel-store-u8-16k"
   "kernel-load-u8-64k"   "kernel-store-u8-64k"
   "kernel-load-u16"      "kernel-store-u16"
   "kernel-load-u16-4k"   "kernel-store-u16-4k"
   "kernel-load-u16-16k"  "kernel-store-u16-16k"
   "kernel-load-u16-64k"  "kernel-store-u16-64k"
   "kernel-load-u32"      "kernel-store-u32"
   "kernel-load-u32-4k"   "kernel-store-u32-4k"
   "kernel-load-u32-16k"  "kernel-store-u32-16k"
   "kernel-load-u32-64k"  "kernel-store-u32-64k"
   "kernel-load-u64"      "kernel-store-u64"
   "kernel-load-u64-4k"   "kernel-store-u64-4k"
   "kernel-load-u64-16k"  "kernel-store-u64-16k"
   "kernel-load-u64-64k"  "kernel-store-u64-64k"
   ;; The machine layer of the slice family: same shape, but `length` and
   ;; `index` count ELEMENTS and the ceiling is an address-space bound.
   "slice-load-u8"        "slice-store-u8"
   "slice-load-u16"       "slice-store-u16"
   "slice-load-u32"       "slice-store-u32"
   "slice-load-u64"       "slice-store-u64"
   ;; A derived window, and the binary try-lock the value runtime holds.
   "kernel-subregion" "kernel-try-lock-u32" "kernel-unlock-u32"
   ;; The general atomics a descriptor ring needs and a try-lock cannot say.
   ;; All six answer with the word memory held BEFORE the operation.
   "kernel-atomic-add-u32" "kernel-atomic-add-u64"
   "kernel-xchg-u32" "kernel-xchg-u64"
   "kernel-cmpxchg-u32" "kernel-cmpxchg-u64"
   ;; Two regions, not one: the only entries whose provenance is not
   ;; "argument 0". The f32 dot product and the fused dequantize-and-dot.
   "kernel-dot-f32"
   "kernel-dequant-dot-q8-0" "kernel-dequant-dot-q4-k" "kernel-dequant-dot-q6-k"

   ;; The CARRIED slice family: a `[:slice T]` value rather than three words.
   ;; A type of the source syntax and of nothing else -- kotoba-sema erases
   ;; these into the eight machine operations above before HIR, so no backend
   ;; and no verifier ever sees one.
   "slice-of-u8" "slice-of-u16" "slice-of-u32" "slice-of-u64"
   "slice-length" "slice-get" "slice-set!" "slice-sub"

   ;; Privileged machine operations. Ring-0 instructions and firmware state:
   ;; each is an effect in at least one direction, none is ever oracled at
   ;; compile time, and a compile-time answer for any of them would be an
   ;; invention the kernel then branches on.
   "kernel-boot-info" "kernel-system-table" "kernel-scratch-region"
   "kernel-read-cr0" "kernel-write-cr0" "kernel-read-cr2"
   "kernel-read-cr3" "kernel-write-cr3" "kernel-read-cr4" "kernel-write-cr4"
   "kernel-read-cs" "kernel-invlpg" "kernel-load-idt" "kernel-load-gdt-tss"
   "kernel-cli" "kernel-sti" "kernel-hlt" "kernel-pause" "kernel-swapgs"
   "kernel-fence-full" "kernel-fence-load" "kernel-fence-store"
   "kernel-rdtsc" "kernel-rdtscp"
   "kernel-out-u8" "kernel-out-u32" "kernel-in-u8" "kernel-in-u32"
   "kernel-read-msr" "kernel-write-msr"
   "kernel-cpuid-eax" "kernel-cpuid-ebx" "kernel-cpuid-ecx" "kernel-cpuid-edx"
   "kernel-xgetbv" "kernel-xsetbv"
   "kernel-page-fault-handler-address" "kernel-page-fault-recovery-handler-address"
   "kernel-configure-page-fault-recovery"
   "kernel-double-fault-handler-address" "kernel-configure-double-fault-ist"
   "kernel-undefined-opcode-handler-address"
   "kernel-rt-timer-handler-address" "kernel-isr-entry-address"
   "kernel-probe-guard-write" "kernel-probe-text-write" "kernel-probe-nx-execute"
   "kernel-probe-recoverable-guard-write" "kernel-probe-double-fault"
   "kernel-load-ptr" "kernel-jump-to"
   "kernel-uefi-call2" "kernel-uefi-call4" "kernel-uefi-call6"
   ;; fwstore: the allocation that answers with an ADDRESS rather than with a
   ;; status, which is what makes the pages it obtained a region-provenance
   ;; root (kotoba-gmir ADR-0030, kotoba-sema ADR-0030). It is not oracled for
   ;; both of this family's reasons at once: it runs firmware code, and its
   ;; answer is a physical page address the caller declares a window over.
   "kernel-uefi-alloc-region"

   "not=" "cond" "case" "if-let" "when-let" "->" "->>"}

 ;; Floating point. Decided by docs/adr/ADR-kotoba-floating-point-on-native.md;
 ;; classified in surface-status as :native-binary32-arithmetic.
 ;;
 ;; These heads are NOT in :arithmetic / :comparisons / :admitted-builtins, and
 ;; that is the point. Floating point is not `+` under another name: it is a
 ;; named operation family with its own width, its own rounding contract, and
 ;; its own NaN behaviour, and giving it the integer spelling would make
 ;; `(+ a b)` mean two different machine operations depending on a type the
 ;; source does not show.
 :floating-point
 {:widths {:f32 :ieee-754-binary32 :f64 :ieee-754-binary64}
  :canonical-spelling :hyphenated-named-operations
  ;; The measurement behind :canonical-spelling: the head `f32-add` resolves
  ;; through kotoba.compiler.frontend's `f32-operations`; `f32+` resolves
  ;; through nothing in that path. `f32+` and its family remain listed in
  ;; :admitted-builtins because that set is the legacy wasm emitter's builtin
  ;; vocabulary -- the neighbours are "i64+", "alloc", "str-ptr" -- and is a
  ;; different surface, not a second name for these.
  :arithmetic #{f32-add f32-sub f32-mul f32-div f32-min f32-max
                f64-add f64-sub f64-mul f64-div f64-min f64-max}
  :unary #{f32-neg f32-abs f32-sqrt f64-neg f64-abs f64-sqrt}
  :comparisons #{f32-eq f32-lt f32-le f32-gt f32-ge f32-unordered
                 f64-eq f64-lt f64-le f64-gt f64-ge f64-unordered}
  :reinterpretation #{f32-from-bits f32-to-bits f64-from-bits f64-to-bits}
  :conversions #{f32-to-f64-exact f64-to-f32-rounded
                 i64-to-f32-rounded i64-to-f64-rounded
                 i64-to-f32-checked i64-to-f64-checked
                 f32-to-i64-checked f32-to-i64-truncating
                 f64-to-i64-checked f64-to-i64-truncating}
  ;; f64 only -- there is no f32 twin of any of these, and inventing one is not
  ;; a naming decision but a numerics decision (each has its own domain bound).
  :bounded-transcendental #{f64-sin-quarter-turn f64-cos-quarter-turn
                            f64-sin-bounded f64-cos-bounded
                            f64-exp-near-zero f64-log-near-one
                            f64-exp-bounded f64-log-bounded f64-atan2-bounded}
  :decimal-parse #{decimal-f64-parse decimal-f64x3-parse}
  :equality "`=` is not admitted between floats. Use the width's own head
             (f32-eq / f64-eq) or compare bit patterns, so that NaN and -0.0
             behave the way IEEE-754 says rather than the way integer equality
             on a word would."
  :rounding {:mode :round-to-nearest-ties-to-even
             :contraction :forbidden
             :fast-math :forbidden}
  :literals {:rule :exact-or-refused
             :note "a decimal literal arrives as a host binary64, so a literal
                    is admitted in an :f32 context only when that binary64
                    round-trips exactly through binary32. 1.5 and 2.0 are
                    admitted; 0.1 is refused, because the value the reader hands
                    over is not the float the author wrote. The explicit
                    spellings are (f64-to-f32-rounded 0.1) and
                    (f32-from-bits 0x3DCCCCCD)."}
  :backend-note "Not every head above reaches every backend. surface-status
                 :native-binary32-arithmetic names the native slice and the six
                 heads refused there, each with its reason."}

 :maturity
